@chapter{SmallTalk Frontend}

This chapter describes the SmallTalk frontend language syntax as implemented in the EightBol compiler.
The SmallTalk frontend provides a subset of Smalltalk-80 syntax suitable for educational and
retrocomputing purposes.

@section{Lexical Elements}

The SmallTalk lexer converts input text into a stream of tokens. Each token has a type and
a value. The following token types are recognized:

@section{Identifier}
An identifier consists of letters, digits, and underscores, starting with a letter.
Identifiers are used for variable names, method names, and class names.

The frontend automatically normalizes identifiers to camelCase (first letter lowercase,
remaining letters unchanged) for consistent AST representation and code generation across
platforms. This normalization occurs during lexical analysis and is transparent to the
source code author.

@example
x                @code{;; remains 'x'}
myVariable       @code{;; remains 'myVariable'}
PrivateData123   @code{;; normalized to 'privateData123'}
MyMethod         @code{;; normalized to 'myMethod'}
CONSTANT_VALUE   @code{;; normalized to 'cONSTANT_VALUE'}
@end example

@subsection{Inputs}
A string of characters forming a valid identifier (letter followed by alphanumerics and underscores).

@subsection{Outputs}
Token of type :IDENT with the identifier string normalized to camelCase.

@subsection{Processing}
The lexer scans consecutive alphanumeric characters and underscores, ensuring the first
character is a letter. Upon recognition, the identifier is converted to camelCase by
lowercasing the first character while preserving the rest of the string.

@subsection{Side-Effects}
Identifiers in generated AST nodes reflect the normalized camelCase form. This ensures
consistent symbol naming across different source code styles.

@section{Number Literals}
Numeric literals support multiple formats for expressing integer and floating-point values:
decimal (base 10), octal (base 8), hexadecimal (base 16), binary (base 2), and dword (16-bit hex).

@subsection{Decimal Numbers}
Standard decimal integers and floating-point numbers, including scientific notation.

@example
42
3.14
1e5
-2.5
0.001
1.23e-4
@end example

@subsection{Hexadecimal Numbers}
Prefixed with @code{0x} (case-insensitive for hex digits).

@example
0x10        @code{;; = 16 decimal}
0xFF        @code{;; = 255 decimal}
0xDEADBEEF  @code{;; = 3735928559 decimal}
@end example

@subsection{Octal Numbers}
Prefixed with @code{0o} (zero followed by lowercase 'o').

@example
0o10        @code{;; = 8 decimal}
0o77        @code{;; = 63 decimal}
0o755       @code{;; = 493 decimal}
@end example

@subsection{Binary Numbers}
Prefixed with @code{0b} (zero followed by lowercase 'b').

@example
0b1010      @code{;; = 10 decimal}
0b11111111  @code{;; = 255 decimal}
0b1         @code{;; = 1 decimal}
@end example

@subsection{Dword Format (16-bit Hexadecimal)}
Prefixed with @code{16r} for explicit 16-bit hex constants, common in Atari platforms.

@example
16r00       @code{;; = 0 decimal}
16rFFFF     @code{;; = 65535 decimal}
16rDEAD     @code{;; = 57005 decimal}
@end example

@subsection{Inputs}
A string representing a valid numeric literal in one of the supported formats.

@subsection{Outputs}
Token of type :NUMBER with the parsed numeric value. Format information is retained for
code generation if needed (e.g., to emit platform-specific constants).

@subsection{Processing}
The lexer recognizes format prefixes (@code{0x}, @code{0o}, @code{0b}, @code{16r}) and
scans appropriate digit characters for each base. Decimal numbers may contain digits,
optional decimal point, and optional exponent notation (@code{e} or @code{E}).

@subsection{Side-Effects}
None.

@section{String Literal}
String literals are enclosed in single quotes.

@example
'hello world'
'Phantasia'
''  ;; empty string
@end example

@subsection{Inputs}
A sequence of characters enclosed in single quotes.

@subsection{Outputs}
Token of type :STRING with the string content (excluding quotes).

@subsection{Processing}
The lexer scans from the opening quote to the closing quote, handling the quote characters
themselves as delimiters.

@subsection{Side-Effects}
None.

@section{Symbol Literal}
Symbol literals are prefixed with a hash mark.

@example
#selector
#at:put:
#nil
@end example

@subsection{Inputs}
A hash mark followed by a valid identifier.

@subsection{Outputs}
Token of type :SYMBOL with the symbol string (including the hash).

@subsection{Processing}
The lexer scans the hash mark and subsequent identifier characters.

@subsection{Side-Effects}
None.

@section{Assignment Operator}
The assignment operator is denoted by :=.

@example
x := 5
collection := Array new
@end example

@subsection{Inputs}
The character sequence :=.

@subsection{Outputs}
Token of type :ASSIGN.

@subsection{Processing}
Exact match of the two-character sequence.

@subsection{Side-Effects}
None.

@section{Period}
The period is used to terminate statements.

@example
x := 1.
Transcript show: 'hello'.
@end example

@subsection{Inputs}
The character . (period).

@subsection{Outputs}
Token of type :PERIOD.

@subsection{Processing}
Exact match of the period character.

@subsection{Side-Effects}
None.

@section{Semicolon}
The semicolon enables cascading of messages to the same receiver.

@example
Transcript
    show: 'hello';
    show: 'world';
    cr.
@end example

@subsection{Inputs}
The character ; (semicolon).

@subsection{Outputs}
Token of type :SEMICOLON.

@subsection{Processing}
Exact match of the semicolon character.

@subsection{Side-Effects}
None.

@section{Colon}
The colon is used in keyword message selectors.

@example
dict at: key put: value
rectangle width: 10 height: 20
@end example

@subsection{Inputs}
The character : (colon).

@subsection{Outputs}
Token of type :COLON.

@subsection{Processing}
Exact match of the colon character.

@subsection{Side-Effects}
None.

@section{Vertical Bar}
The vertical bar is used to declare temporary variables in blocks and to separate block
elements.

@example
[:x | x + 1]
| temp1 temp2 |
temp1 := 1.
temp2 := temp1 * 2.
^[x | x > 10]  ;; block with parameter
@end example

@subsection{Inputs}
The character | (vertical bar).

@subsection{Outputs}
Token of type :VBAR.

@subsection{Processing}
Exact match of the vertical bar character.

@subsection{Side-Effects}
None.

@section{Left Bracket}
The left bracket begins a block literal.

@example
[:n | n factorial]
[Transcript show: 'Hello']
#endif  ;; conditional block
@end example

@subsection{Inputs}
The character [ (left bracket).

@subsection{Outputs}
Token of type :LBRACKET.

@subsection{Processing}
Exact match of the left bracket character.

@subsection{Side-Effects}
None.

@section{Right Bracket}
The right bracket ends a block literal.

@example
[:n | n factorial]
[Transcript show: 'Hello']
#endif
@end example

@subsection{Inputs}
The character ] (right bracket).

@subsection{Outputs}
Token of type :RBRACKET.

@subsection{Processing}
Exact match of the right bracket character.

@subsection{Side-Effects}
None.

@section{Left Parenthesis}
The left parenthesis is used for grouping expressions and parameter lists.

@example
(x + y) * z
Array new: (10 * 5)
(obj isNil) ifTrue: [^nil]
@end example

@subsection{Inputs}
The character ( (left parenthesis).

@subsection{Outputs}
Token of type :LPAREN.

@subsection{Processing}
Exact match of the left parenthesis character.

@subsection{Side-Effects}
None.

@section{Right Parenthesis}
The right parenthesis closes grouping expressions and parameter lists.

@example
(x + y) * z
Array new: (10 * 5)
(obj isNil) ifTrue: [^nil]
@end example

@subsection{Inputs}
The character ) (right parenthesis).

@subsection{Outputs}
Token of type :RPAREN.

@subsection{Processing}
Exact match of the right parenthesis character.

@subsection{Side-Effects}
None.

@section{Operators}
Various operators are available for arithmetic, comparison, and logical operations.

@example
+  ;; addition
-  ;; subtraction
*  ;; multiplication
/  ;; division
=  ;; equivalence
== ;; identity
~= ;; difference
>  ;; greater than
<  ;; less than
>= ;; greater than or equal
<= ;; less than or equal
@  ;; array creation point
@end example

@subsection{Inputs}
Single or double character sequences representing operators.

@subsection{Outputs}
Token types corresponding to each operator (e.g., :PLUS for +, :TIMES for *, etc.).

@subsection{Processing}
Exact match of operator character sequences.

@subsection{Side-Effects}
None.

@section{Number Format Reference}
The SmallTalk frontend supports multiple number literal formats for convenience across
different programming contexts. All formats parse to their integer or floating-point
values for use in expressions and assignments.

@subsection{Decimal Format}
Standard base-10 integers and floating-point numbers.

@example
@code{42} — forty-two
@code{3.14} — pi approximation
@code{1e5} — 100,000 in scientific notation
@code{-2.5} — negative decimal
@end example

@subsection{Hexadecimal Format (0x prefix)}
Base-16 numbers for bitwise operations and memory addresses (common in assembly contexts).

@example
@code{0x10} — sixteen
@code{0xFF} — 255 (maximum unsigned byte)
@code{0xDEADBEEF} — valid 32-bit hex constant
@end example

@subsection{Octal Format (0o prefix)}
Base-8 numbers (legacy Unix file permissions, Atari display lists).

@example
@code{0o10} — eight
@code{0o77} — sixty-three
@code{0o755} — file permissions rwxr-xr-x
@end example

@subsection{Binary Format (0b prefix)}
Base-2 numbers for bit manipulation and flag definitions.

@example
@code{0b1010} — ten (0b patterns)
@code{0b11111111} — 255 (all bits set)
@code{0b1} — one
@end example

@subsection{Dword Format (16r prefix)}
Explicit 16-bit hexadecimal constant (Atari platform convention).

@example
@code{16r00} — zero
@code{16rFFFF} — 65,535 (16-bit maximum)
@code{16rDEAD} — 57,005 (test value)
@end example

@subsection{Inputs}
Any numeric literal in one of the supported formats.

@subsection{Outputs}
Token of type :NUMBER with the parsed numeric value.

@subsection{Processing}
The lexer identifies format prefixes and dispatches to appropriate radix parsers.
Prefix detection happens before general number scanning, allowing format-specific
digit validation (e.g., binary only accepts 0 and 1).

@subsection{Side-Effects}
None.

@section{Whitespace}
Spaces, tabs, and newlines are ignored except to separate tokens.

@example
x:=1  ;; equivalent to x := 1
ifTrue[
    x := 2
]     ;; whitespace around brackets is ignored
@end example

@subsection{Inputs}
Whitespace characters (space, tab, newline).

@subsection{Outputs}
No tokens produced.

@subsection{Processing}
Whitespace is skipped between tokens.

@subsection{Side-Effects}
None.

@section{Comments}
Comments are not supported in the SmallTalk frontend.

@section{Syntax Elements}

Building upon the lexical elements, the SmallTalk frontend supports the following
syntactic constructs.

@section{Assignment Statement}
Assigns a value to a variable.

@example
x := 5
obj := Sprite new
count := count + 1
@end example

@subsection{Inputs}
Variable identifier, assignment operator (:=), and an expression.

@subsection{Outputs}
AST node representing the assignment operation.

@subsection{Processing}
The parser recognizes the pattern: variable := expression

@subsection{Side-Effects}
The variable is bound to the result of evaluating the expression.

@section{Message Send Expression}
Sends a message to an object (receiver) with optional arguments.

@example
Transcript show: 'hello'  ;; keyword message
x + y                     ;; binary message
obj size                  ;; unary message
dict at: key put: value   ;; multiple keywords
@end example

@subsection{Inputs}
A receiver expression followed by a message selector and optional arguments.

@subsection{Outputs}
AST node representing the message send operation.

@subsection{Processing}
The parser recognizes three message patterns:
- Unary: receiver messageSelector (e.g., obj size)
- Binary: receiver operator argument (e.g., x + y)
- Keyword: receiver keyword1: arg1 keyword2: arg2 ... (e.g., dict at: key put: value)

@subsection{Side-Effects}
The message is looked up in the receiver's class and the corresponding method is executed
with the provided arguments.

@section{Block Literal}
A block literal represents an anonymous function that can be executed later.

@example
[:x | x * x]                     ;; block with parameter
[Transcript show: 'Hello']       ;; block with no parameters
[:a :b | a > b]                  ;; block with multiple parameters
[ | temp | temp := 10 ]          ;; block with temporary variables
@end example

@subsection{Inputs}
An opening bracket, optional parameter list (identifiers separated by spaces, each
prefixed with colon), optional vertical bar and temporary variable declarations,
and a sequence of expressions, ending with a closing bracket.

@subsection{Outputs}
AST node representing the block literal.

@subsection{Processing}
The parser recognizes the pattern: [ [:param1 :param2...] [| temp1 temp2...] | expressions ]

@subsection{Side-Effects}
When the block is executed via value or related messages, the enclosed expressions are
evaluated in the context of the block's parameters and temporaries.

@section{Expression}
An expression represents a computation that produces a value.

@example
42
x
x + y
[ :n | n factorial ] value
obj perform: #selector
@end example

@subsection{Inputs}
Any valid combination of literals, variables, message sends, and blocks according to
SmallTalk precedence rules.

@subsection{Outputs}
Value resulting from the expression evaluation.

@subsection{Processing}
Expressions are parsed according to operator precedence:
1. Unary messages
2. Binary messages (left to right)
3. Keyword messages
4. Literals, variables, blocks, and parenthesized expressions (highest precedence)

@subsection{Side-Effects}
Expression evaluation may have side effects depending on the operations performed
(message sends, assignments within blocks, etc.).

@section{Cascade}
The semicolon allows sending multiple messages to the same receiver without
repeating the receiver.

@example
Transcript
    show: 'hello';
    show: 'world';
    cr.
    
obj
    color: Color red;
    borderWidth: 2;
    drawRect: (0 @ 0 extent: 100 @ 100).
@end example

@subsection{Inputs}
A receiver expression followed by a message, then zero or more occurrences of:
semicolon followed by a message.

@subsection{Outputs}
AST nodes for each message send in the cascade.

@subsection{Processing}
The parser transforms a cascade into individual message sends to the same receiver.

@subsection{Side-Effects}
Each message in the cascade is executed sequentially, with the receiver being the
same object for all messages.

@section{Method Definition}
Method definitions appear in class definitions and specify the behavior of objects.

@example
drawOn: aCanvas
    aCanvas
        fillRect: self bounds
        borderWidth: self borderWidth
        drawRect: self bounds.
        
initialize
    super initialize.
    color: Color blue.
    borderWidth: 1.
@end example

@subsection{Inputs}
A message selector (with optional parameters) followed by a sequence of expressions
ending with a period.

@subsection{Outputs}
AST node representing the method definition.

@subsection{Processing}
The parser recognizes method definition syntax within class contexts.

@subsection{Side-Effects}
When the method is compiled, it becomes part of the class's method dictionary and
can be invoked via message sends.

@section{Control Structures}
Control structures are implemented as messages sent to Boolean objects or blocks.

@example
(x > 0) ifTrue: [^x] ifFalse: [^-x]
 SmallTalk does not have dedicated syntax for control structures; they are
 implemented via the standard message sending mechanism.
 
Common control patterns include:
- ifTrue:ifFalse: - conditional execution
- whileTrue: - repeated execution while condition holds
- to:do: - counting loop
- do: - execute block with each collection element
@end example

@subsection{Inputs}
Boolean expressions and block literals as arguments to control messages.

@subsection{Outputs}
Depends on the specific control message.

@subsection{Processing}
Control messages evaluate their block arguments conditionally or repeatedly.

@subsection{Side-Effects}
Depends on the controlled execution of block arguments.

@section{Reader Macro Analogies}

While SmallTalk does not have reader macros like Lisp, certain lexical elements
serve similar purposes by providing alternate representations for common constructs:

@table @asis
@item Symbol Literals (#symbol)
Provide a concise way to reference unique identifiers, similar to how reader macros
might provide shorthand for complex forms.

@item Array Literals (#(1 2 3))
Though not fully implemented in this frontend, the # syntax serves as a reader-like
construct for literal data structures.

@item Block Literals ([ ... ])
Provide a literal representation of executable code, analogous to how reader macros
might quote or delay evaluation of forms.
@end table

These constructs enable metaprogramming techniques by treating code as data and
vice versa, fulfilling a similar role to reader macros in other languages.

@section{AST Node Processing}

@subsection{Identifier Normalization}

The SmallTalk frontend converts all identifiers to camelCase canonical form during AST production.
This normalization is applied consistently to variable names, method selectors, and block parameters.
The transformation is transparent to source code but ensures consistent handling across all code generation
backends (6502, Z80, m68k, etc.).

@subsubsection{camelCase Transformation Rules}

@enumerate
@item The first character of the identifier is converted to lowercase.
@item All subsequent characters are preserved as-is (maintaining case from source).
@item This transformation is applied during lexical analysis before parsing.
@item AST nodes contain the normalized identifier, not the original source text.
@end enumerate

@subsubsection{Examples}

@table @asis
@item MyClass
becomes @code{myClass}

@item initialize
remains @code{initialize}

@item CONSTANT_NAME
becomes @code{cONSTANT_NAME}

@item atKeyPutValue
remains @code{atKeyPutValue}

@item DoSomething
becomes @code{doSomething}
@end table